WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:04.890
Narrator: Recognize these characters?

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:05.993
These eyeballs?

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.537
This face?

00:00:09.537 --> 00:00:12.840
They're all made by the
same guy, Stan Winston,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.980
and there's a way to learn
exactly how he brought

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.870
these practical-effects legends to life.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:21.210
Practical effects often
get overshadowed by CGI,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.780
which can do the seemingly impossible.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.860
One school, however,
thinks that special effects

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.760
are here to stay and is
helping teach the craft

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.760
to the next generation of artists.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.240
The Stan Winston School
is an online school

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.520
based in Los Angeles, where a wide array

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.250
of Hollywood special-effects artists

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.070
have taught character creation

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.360
to over 20,000 students
since its founding.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.210
Its online tutorials can be watched

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.400
either live or at your convenience.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.030
Classes instruct viewers
how to do everything

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:57.350
from putting feathers on a fake bird,

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.030
to making a mask, to creating
a convincing monster.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.814
Erich Grey Litoff: Online
education allows you

00:01:02.814 --> 00:01:04.139
to have the tools at your hands

00:01:04.139 --> 00:01:06.058
to learn on your own time.

00:01:06.058 --> 00:01:08.000
Narrator: The school's
named after this guy,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.930
Stan Winston, and his
teachings and creations

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.260
greatly influence
everything about the school.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.210
He was one of Hollywood's most legendary

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.710
special-effects artists
and a pioneer in the field.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.560
The school was launched
by his son, Matt Winston,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.200
and son-in-law, Erich Grey Litoff.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.308
Matt Winston: One of Stan's mantras was,

00:01:27.308 --> 00:01:28.733
do what you love,

00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:30.016
follow your passion,

00:01:30.016 --> 00:01:31.730
and success will come.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:32.950
Narrator: Winston came to Hollywood

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.210
with dreams of becoming an actor.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.740
When that didn't work out,

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.310
he ended up in the world
of special effects.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:43.002
Even if you don't know his
name, you know his work.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.440
Perhaps his proudest achievement

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.600
was the "Jurassic Park" franchise,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.700
in which he built dinosaurs
both big and small.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.308
Grey Litoff: So, this
would be one of the claws,

00:02:09.308 --> 00:02:12.033
screen-used claw from the
T. rex in "Jurassic Park."

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:14.540
Narrator: Along with his
many groundbreaking effects,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.460
Winston also managed to
film a lot of his work,

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.480
which the school has in its
archive and often posts online.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.040
The school itself is filled
with some of Winston's classics.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.490
Stan's influences show up

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.170
in many of the things
it builds for classes.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.003
Grey Litoff: These are raptor eyes

00:02:30.003 --> 00:02:31.603
from the "Jurassic Park" series.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.090
These are hand-painted, clear resin on top

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.410
of the painted eye, and, you
can see, to give the illusion,

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.040
there's actually a little bit of depth

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.560
between where the paint is
and the urethane is on there.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.480
Narrator: Teachers will show you

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.510
how to make realistic
fake eyeballs in class.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.100
Winston also built the metal arm

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.630
for T-X in "Terminator
3: Rise of the Machines."

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.130
In one of the classes,
beginners were taught

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:58.630
how to build a very simple
working mechanical arm.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:00.720
Winston: Using just plumbing parts

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.100
and stuff you could go
find at the hardware store.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.270
Now, definitely, it's very
crude, but the principles

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:11.090
can be applied to a much more
advanced mechanical hand.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.330
Narrator: And this silicone rod puppet,

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.050
which looks a lot like this one

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.870
that was designed for the
movie "Small Soldiers."

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.020
Grey Litoff: And now, with CGI technology,

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:20.861
it's easier than ever

00:03:20.861 --> 00:03:23.530
to remove the puppeteer, remove the rod.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.450
Narrator: Sometimes, these old pieces

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.450
are actually used in lessons.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.940
In one lesson, teachers
use this moving tentacle,

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.630
which runs on cables
attached to a controller

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.620
that was used to move
and control the dinosaurs

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:37.830
in one of the "Jurassic Park" movies.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.130
The school emphasizes Stan's
philosophy for effects,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.170
especially that a lot of influences,

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.070
even for sci-fi and fantasy
puppets and effects,

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.340
should be rooted in the
real world and nature.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.010
It's why "Predator" might
have looked so real to you

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.850
and also how, in "Instinct,"
they were able to get away

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:57.800
with a gorilla that was actually
just an animatronic mask

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.010
worn by a guy in a gorilla suit.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:01.585
Another major lesson that a budding

00:04:01.585 --> 00:04:03.834
special-effects artist must learn:

00:04:03.834 --> 00:04:06.350
avoiding the uncanny
valley, when something

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.830
appears a little too
frighteningly realistic.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.150
Winston: If you actually
have a puppet on set,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.980
physics is now working with you,

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.810
and natural laws are now working with you.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:19.100
The same light is landing on
the puppet as is on the actor,

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.690
so that heightens the level of realism.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:23.820
Narrator: The school's also a means

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.160
of teaching and preserving these methods.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.770
A lot of movie special effects, sadly,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.670
were made just for use on a given movie

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.530
and didn't have a long shelf life.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.840
Winston: Oftentimes, the
materials you need to use

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.020
to make sure something is totally
lifelike are very fragile.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:45.020
Back in the old days of props
and effects and puppets,

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.470
people didn't really see
a value beyond the filming

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.610
of the movie, and so many of those things

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.792
just disappeared or were thrown away.

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:57.360
More and more, studios
are taking great efforts

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.610
to preserve these creations,

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.120
and that's fantastic for everyone

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.928
because this really is Hollywood history,

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:07.245
and it's our duty and our honor

00:05:07.245 --> 00:05:10.350
to play our part in
helping to preserve it.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:11.420
Narrator: And even though movies today

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.830
are seemingly dominated by CGI,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.530
practical effects aren't going anywhere.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:20.140
Stan Winston embraced CGI,
and in movies he worked on,

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.290
it often went hand in hand
with practical effects.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:27.023
For instance, in "Jurassic
Park," in close-ups

00:05:27.023 --> 00:05:30.010
or any shots involving
interactions with humans,

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.260
the dinosaurs were usually puppets.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.780
For wider shots involving more movement,

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.780
that was typically CGI.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.640
Winston: He always believed that the job

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:43.900
of an effects artist is to be invisible.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.280
You never want an audience

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.910
to walk out of a theater
going, "Great effects."

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:51.430
You want them to say, "Great
story, great characters."

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.160
And if you could achieve
that, you succeeded.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:59.440
For the famous shot in "Jurassic Park"

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.080
where the T. rex leans down
and Lexi shines her flashlight

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.053
into his eye and the pupil dilates,

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.040
a special device was created
to create that dilating action.

